      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Cost and Lifespan"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Asphalt Shingles: Lower initial investment, faster installation, but requires replacement sooner due to UV degradation and high temperature swings in Utah.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              1,
              2
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Metal Roofing: Higher upfront expense (often 2x to 3x the cost of shingles), but serves as a long-term investment with a low life-cycle cost if you plan to stay in your home for decades."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Weather Performance in Utah"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Snow and Ice: Metal roofs smoothly shed heavy snowpacks typical of Wasatch Back or mountain winters, though snow retention guards are required over entryways. Asphalt holds more snow and ice, increasing ice dam risks if attic ventilation is inadequate.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              3,
              4
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Wind and Fire: Quality metal systems handle high canyon gusts and resist sparks/wildfires with a Class A fire rating. Modern architectural asphalt shingles withstand strong winds when properly installed, but are petroleum-based.",
            "reference_indexes": [
              5,
              6
            ]
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Hail and Sun: High-altitude Utah sun wears down shingle granules over time, while metal reflects radiant heat and resists cracking, though large hail can cause cosmetic dents on softer metal panels."
          }
        ],
        "reference_indexes": [
          7,
          8
        ]
